hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  Peter Singer begins his essay the  biological basis of ethics with a short  passage by Henry Sidgwick representing  not just the utilitarian tradition but  but a lot of other traditions in  philosophy in making a certain set of  claims about duties or moral obligations  and he starts out saying this we should  all agree each of us is bound to show  kindness to his parents and spouse and  children and to other kinsmen in a less  degree and then he goes on from there to  describe other people who we have  similar obligations towards but notice  what is is really front and center here  as singer is going to point out parents  and children and spouse the very close  intimate family relationships and he  doesn't say that everybody has a great  family or that you know everybody is  going to get along with the people in  their family but he is saying there is a  recognized moral obligation based on a  sort of closeness that's really quite  arbitrary you could say that spouses  choose each other at least in some cases  although they often don't know precisely  who they're choosing until they get to  know them a little bit better but  children don't choose their parents and  quite frankly parents don't really  choose their children either because  children turn out very differently than  you that you ever expect so you know  there's this kind of strangeness to how  an arbitrary set of biological relations  granted with all sorts of cultural  status involved as well should be so  important in ethics and singer thinks  that actually this is not that  surprising at all so he begins by  talking about in the section kin  selection in human ethics and he says  the passage that I quoted is a  description of the principles Rek  regulating the duty of benevolence as  that Duty was generally understood at  that time it gives a graduated list of  those to whom we should be kind which  fits neatly here is where we gets a  singer's own perspective with  sociobiological theories what our  associate biological theories their  theories of ethics that say that our  biology plays an important role so that  selection operating on a number of  different levels both for the individual  and within groups is important favors  some sorts of behaviors disfavors others  leading to their replication and  reproduction and so on and so on and  it's it's sociobiological in that it  recognizes that with a human animal  there is an incredible amount of culture  involved as well so he goes on to point  out that in some ethics at least there  will be some where it's not the case and  in human behavior generally we have this  phenomenon that we call kin altruism  which is both the actual behavior of  favoring and showing benevolence towards  those who we are connected with  kinship ties however we conceived of  those however we mark and code those in  whatever society that we're in and also  there's a sense of aught a perceived  moral obligation there as well so it's  not purely descriptive it's also  prescriptive as well and you know we  could think about what our typical  instances of this looking after children  the idea that not always you know  honored by everybody if you produce  children you ought to take care of them  and you can find this in all sorts of  cultures and all sorts of ethical  theories and then also you know  supporting the family as a whole however  family is understood up and supporting  the members of the family so singer  talks about how in late Victorian  England there could be you know here we  go duties to parents alongside duties to  children and wives with duties to eight  brothers and sisters and then duties to  more distant relatives like cousins  things like that you know a great  example of this is when somebody strikes  it rich  and suddenly they have lots of cousins  some of whom may not actually be related  to them showing up holding out their  hand and saying where is my generosity  from you why are they doing that it's  not purely based on whatever I can get  out of somebody there's there's a sense  that there is some sort of obligation  there however it misplaced it might be  now singer says some you know  anthropologists and sociologists and  other social scientists would say that  this is not really biological this is  just cultural and he says that's not  really that big of a problem we can have  a flexible enough theory to say it's  probably got components of both every  society that we look at has kinship  structures they may arrange them  differently but they still have the same  and  even in our late modern society with  conceptions like say blended families or  you know widespread adoption and all  sorts of other things that that go on  we're still working with the same basic  ideas here in the same basic structure  of kin selection or kin altruism now  what is it to be altruistic it means to  be other center to focus on the good or  the needs of the other and perhaps to  make sacrifices of one's own interests  in the in the case that that it's  required now he goes on and he talks  about a few other important aspects of  this one is that we see that people tend  to favor kin over other people who from  an ethical perspective could be viewed  as having an equal claim on their  resources or their generosity what we  might call needy and deserving others so  he goes on and he he says think of our  reaction to news of a famine in Africa  those who care at all  may send a donation to one of the  agencies trying to help ten dollars or  fifty dollars even perhaps a hundred  dollars any more would be a rare act of  generosity by the standards of society  but those of us fortunate enough to live  in Western Europe North America  Australia or Japan regularly spend as  much or more on holidays new clothes or  presents for our children so if we cared  he says about the lives and welfare of  strangers in Africa as we do about our  own welfare and the ad of our children  why would we spend money on these  non-essential items for ourselves  instead of spending it to save lives we  could be buying mosquito nets or  important medicines or paying for a  school or a road or we could go down the  line with a whole series of ORS here why  don't we do that well because we tend to  you favor those who are related to us or  those who we perceive of as related to  us he goes on he says I have written and  lectured on the subject of overseas aid  arguing our affluence puts us under an  obligation to do much more than we're  doing to help people in real need at the  popular level here's where singer notes  there's there's a tendency here the most  frequent response is that we should look  after our own poor first among  philosophers sometimes replies are in a  more sophisticated manner I've been told  while we should do more to prevent  poverty we should do nothing  inconsistent here's where we get to the  kin altruism with our obligation to do  the best for our children and as singer  points out this obligation to do the  best for our children involves all sorts  of expenses that oftentimes are quite  high and rather arbitrary and don't seem  to get an awful lot of bang for the buck  in terms of human welfare what's an  example of this sending them to  expensive private schools a great  example of this is you look at our own  Congress our legislators you know they  they could be sending their kids to  schools in their districts especially  the ones who you know say maintain a  residence in the capital they don't then  certainly not to public schools they  tend to favor private schools right why  because you know private schools are  typically better off than public schools  they also tend to be very expensive or  he has another example here that you  might find a little bit funny buying  them 10 speed bicycles this is probably  a little bit dated they're buying them  gaming consoles the kids really need  gaming consoles or the latest fashion or  any of these things no not really but  people purchase these things for their  kids anyway instead of devoting those  resources even to the poor in their own  neighborhoods let alone the poor  elsewhere why is that the case  well he says that we have a preference  for our own which he says is  understandable in terms of our  evolutionary history we seek in altruism  in other animals and we it's not  surprising that we also see it in our  own and so he says that family ties are  based on what he calls an exclusive and  discriminating loyalty which sets the  members of one's family more or less  apart from others this is again what kin  altruism is and does he also brings up  proposals and even attempt to try to do  away with what we can call the  institution of the family the locus of  kin altruism he brings up Plato's  Republic and Plato's argument in the  Republic is so long as people are  thinking primarily in terms of their own  family they're not going to be a unified  society and the goal is to try to get  everybody to have the kind of affection  that we would have within a family and  the sort of altruism for the members of  our society what he's calling later  singer group altruism the goal is to do  that by eliminating the institution of  the family and it would be based on age  class so you just call everybody who's  older than you who is a male father and  everybody who is older than you mother  unless they're old enough to be  grandfather and grandmother perhaps  there's you know aunts and uncles or  things like that as well but we don't to  get into the weeds with that now Plato's  Republic was never put into practice at  least not successfully but he also talks  about the Israeli kibbutz 'm the idea  was put into practice there you would  have a collective and there would be  children born within it but then the  children are being mothered and fathered  being parented by a wide range of people  in there's an  hemp to try to minimize the amount of  family time and and separate households  and things like that and he noticed he  points out that this didn't really work  it says what for whatever reason once  the kibitz room became established in  the twin dangers of starvation and Arab  attack receded the ideal of unity proved  insufficient to maintain the original  intensity of communal feeling they have  survived but they had to come to terms  with the family couple spend more time  in their apartments often taking their  meals their children are with their  parents for much of their free time and  often sleep in the family apartment  rather than the children's house it's no  longer frowned upon for couples to sit  next to each other on all public  occasions children once again call their  parents mother and father and he brings  up other attempts to do this sort of  thing it's very difficult to eradicate  this family feeling now there are like  we pointed out a little bit earlier some  tensions between what's good for the  family and what's good for the larger  society so the last thing that Peter  Singer considers is this interesting  question well why do societies why do  they allow they tolerate and going  beyond that actually promote and praise  the family when it seems like family  loyalty could create people who are  going to be very good to their mothers  fathers children spouses siblings but  not so good to other people in society  so he says that the answer may lie not  just in the universality and strength of  family feeling but also in the benefits  to society as a whole that come from  families taking care of themselves  what are these benefits he says when  families see that children are fed kept  clean and sheltered so taking care of  children that the sicker nursed and the  elderly cared for  they are being led by what singers  calling bonds of natural affection or it  could be duty as well if they don't like  their family that much to do what would  otherwise fall upon the community itself  and either would not be done at all or  would require labor unmotivated by  natural impulses so the family because  of the way kin altruism works gets  things done that need to be done for a  functioning society and its members  granted there's going to be some people  are gonna fall through the cracks but  they can be brought into other families  as well they can be adopted they can be  taken care of and he says that in a  large modern community doing all these  sorts of things would require an  expensive and impersonal bureaucracy so  it may be more cost-effective and then  it goes on and says given the much  greater intensity of family feeling  compared with the degree of concern we  have for the welfare of strangers which  is not very much frankly ethical rules  which accept a degree of partiality  towards the interests of one's family  maybe the best means of promoting the  welfare of all families and thus of the  entire community  so kin altruism can be harnessed to  handle a lot of things that otherwise  would fall upon society to take care of  so it would have a sort of you know  pragmatic justification that's what  Peter Singer has to say about this  powerful impulse of kin altruism in this  this essay  you
 

